WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.096 --> 00:00:16.526
Our took place so quickly that I shivered all over.

2
00:00:17.367 --> 00:00:18.848
Who were our captors?

3
00:00:19.708 --> 00:00:24.072
They were undoubtedly some new sort of pirates, who explored the sea in their own way.

4
00:00:25.012 --> 00:00:29.236
The narrow hatch closed above us and we were surrounded by complete darkness.

5
00:00:30.164 --> 00:00:35.286
My eyes, dazzled by the brightness outside the vessel, couldn't adjust to the lack of light inside.

6
00:00:36.207 --> 00:00:37.907
The men forced us down a metal ladder.

7
00:00:38.588 --> 00:00:40.508
At the bottom of the ladder, a door opened.

8
00:00:41.489 --> 00:00:45.350
We were shoved into a room and the door immediately shut behind us with a bang.

9
00:00:46.311 --> 00:00:50.492
The three of us were alone, and I couldn't even begin to imagine where we were.

10
00:00:51.393 --> 00:00:54.994
My eyes were still not able to discern even the faintest glimmer of light.

11
00:00:55.014 --> 00:00:57.855
"These people are the worst hosts I've ever met!"

12
00:00:58.276 --> 00:00:58.816
shouted Ned.

13
00:00:59.676 --> 00:01:01.838
"Fortunately I still have my knife with me."

14
00:01:01.858 --> 00:01:05.821
"Calm yourself, my friend," said Conseil.

15
00:01:05.841 --> 00:01:08.162
"Yes, Ned," I said.

16
00:01:08.182 --> 00:01:11.745
"Don't make the situation worse with senseless violence.

17
00:01:12.666 --> 00:01:17.670
Perhaps we can get our captors to listen to us, but first let's try to figure out where

18
00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:18.150
we are."

19
00:01:19.171 --> 00:01:20.972
I began to grope my way around the room.

20
00:01:21.793 --> 00:01:26.056
In five steps I came to a wall made of iron plates bolted together.

21
00:01:26.988 --> 00:01:31.992
Turning around, I ran into a wooden table around which were arranged several stools.

22
00:01:32.933 --> 00:01:37.977
The floor of this prison was concealed under thick mats that deadened the sound of footsteps.

23
00:01:38.918 --> 00:01:42.041
The bare walls revealed no trace of window or door.

24
00:01:42.821 --> 00:01:45.764
I guessed that the cabin measured about twenty feet by ten.

25
00:01:46.785 --> 00:01:52.149
Half an hour had passed without any change in our situation when suddenly a bright light went on.

26
00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:55.172
I squeezed my eyes tight against the light.

27
00:01:56.556 --> 00:02:01.537
When I slowly opened them, I saw that the source of this brightness was an electric light in

28
00:02:01.557 --> 00:02:02.138
the ceiling.

29
00:02:03.078 --> 00:02:08.059
The sudden illumination of the cabin allowed me to examine it more carefully, but it only contained the

30
00:02:08.099 --> 00:02:09.460
table and five stools.

31
00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:14.581
We couldn't hear a sound; everything seemed dead onboard this mysterious vessel.

32
00:02:15.521 --> 00:02:20.263
Was it floating on the surface of the sea or had it dived into the ocean's depths?

33
00:02:21.083 --> 00:02:21.903
I had no idea.

34
00:02:22.863 --> 00:02:26.581
But then we did hear a sound: the noise of bolts sliding.

35
00:02:27.341 --> 00:02:29.663
A door opened in a wall and two men appeared.

36
00:02:30.763 --> 00:02:34.986
One was short and very muscular with thick black hair and a mustache.

37
00:02:35.886 --> 00:02:40.809
The second man was tall with a large forehead, straight nose, and fine teeth.

38
00:02:41.749 --> 00:02:46.252
One peculiar feature was that his cold, black eyes were set rather far apart.

39
00:02:47.192 --> 00:02:52.615
After observing him for a few moments, I decided that he was a calm, self-confident man full of

40
00:02:52.735 --> 00:02:53.696
energy and courage.

41
00:02:55.124 --> 00:02:59.688
But whether this man was thirty-five or fifty years of age, I could not say.

42
00:03:00.629 --> 00:03:05.593
The two strangers wore fur caps and boots, and their clothes were made from a fabric that I

43
00:03:05.693 --> 00:03:06.594
couldn't identify.

44
00:03:07.515 --> 00:03:12.539
The taller man, who was evidently the captain of the vessel, carefully examined us in silence.

45
00:03:13.580 --> 00:03:17.824
Then he said something to his lieutenant in a language that I couldn't understand.

46
00:03:18.684 --> 00:03:22.728
The second man replied to him and stared at me as if waiting for me to speak.

47
00:03:23.668 --> 00:03:27.649
I said in French that I didn't know his language, but he didn't seem to understand me.

48
00:03:27.669 --> 00:03:35.311
"Master, if you were to tell our story, perhaps these gentlemen might understand some words," suggested Conseil.

49
00:03:36.272 --> 00:03:39.173
I began to describe our adventures onboard the Abraham Lincoln.

50
00:03:40.133 --> 00:03:46.315
Then I introduced us as Professor Pierre Aronnax, his servant Conseil, and Mr.

51
00:03:46.395 --> 00:03:47.835
Ned Land, the harpooner.

52
00:03:49.315 --> 00:03:54.113
The men listened to us quietly, but there was nothing in their faces to indicate that they'd understood

53
00:03:54.153 --> 00:03:54.533
a word.

54
00:03:54.553 --> 00:03:58.175
"Perhaps they understand English," I said to Ned.

55
00:03:58.195 --> 00:04:00.156
"You speak it better than I do.

56
00:04:00.676 --> 00:04:01.276
Give it a try."

57
00:04:02.357 --> 00:04:06.218
Once more Ned told our story in English, but with the same result.

58
00:04:07.059 --> 00:04:10.060
Next Conseil tried in German with no success.

59
00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:12.901
I even attempted to tell it in Latin.

60
00:04:13.621 --> 00:04:18.163
The two strangers exchanged some words in their unknown language and left the room.

61
00:04:19.283 --> 00:04:20.224
Ned was furious.

62
00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:25.882
"We spoke to those rogues in French, English, German, and Latin, and they didn't have the politeness to

63
00:04:26.022 --> 00:04:28.943
answer!" "Calm yourself, Ned," I said.

64
00:04:28.963 --> 00:04:30.904
"Anger won't do us any good."

65
00:04:30.924 --> 00:04:33.205
"But don't you see, Professor?

66
00:04:33.545 --> 00:04:35.386
We'll die of hunger in this iron prison!"

67
00:04:35.826 --> 00:04:36.326
he replied.

68
00:04:36.346 --> 00:04:39.868
"We won't starve," said Conseil calmly.

69
00:04:39.888 --> 00:04:41.968
"We can hold out for some time yet."

70
00:04:43.289 --> 00:04:45.570
"My friends, we must not despair," I said.

71
00:04:45.590 --> 00:04:51.965
"Meanwhile I can't decide if our captors are from Spain, Turkey, Arabia, or India.

72
00:04:52.285 --> 00:04:54.166
Their language is quite incomprehensible."

73
00:04:55.106 --> 00:04:59.769
As I said these words, the door opened and a steward entered with fresh clothes for us.

74
00:05:00.609 --> 00:05:03.131
While we hurried to dress, the steward set the table.

75
00:05:03.151 --> 00:05:05.052
"Now that's more like it!"

76
00:05:05.432 --> 00:05:06.032
said Conseil.

77
00:05:06.052 --> 00:05:08.034
"Bah!" said Ned.

78
00:05:08.054 --> 00:05:11.015
"What do you suppose they eat here—tortoise liver?"

79
00:05:11.936 --> 00:05:14.477
The place settings were elegant and in perfect taste.

80
00:05:15.438 --> 00:05:20.545
We were undoubtedly dealing with civilized people, and if it weren't for the electric light, I could imagine

81
00:05:20.585 --> 00:05:23.027
we were dining in one of the finest hotels in Europe.

82
00:05:23.947 --> 00:05:30.193
All the dishes and silverware were inscribed with the letter N and a Latin motto: Mobilis in mobili.

83
00:05:30.213 --> 00:05:32.454
"What does that mean, Professor?"

84
00:05:32.875 --> 00:05:34.856
asked Ned, running a finger over the letters.

85
00:05:34.876 --> 00:05:39.300
"N is undoubtedly the captain's initial," I said.

86
00:05:39.320 --> 00:05:46.486
"And the inscription means mobile within the mobile element or in this case moving through water."

87
00:05:47.387 --> 00:05:50.734
We didn't talk much after that, being too busy devouring our food.

88
00:05:51.635 --> 00:05:56.299
I recognized some fish dishes, but I wasn't sure if some of the other things we ate were

89
00:05:56.519 --> 00:05:57.560
animals or vegetables.

90
00:05:58.501 --> 00:06:01.023
At least our hosts were not going to let us starve.

91
00:06:02.044 --> 00:06:06.548
After eating, my two companions settled themselves on the floor of the cabin and were soon asleep.

92
00:06:07.469 --> 00:06:10.832
But I lay awake wondering where we were and with whom.

93
00:06:11.833 --> 00:06:15.036
At one point I thought the vessel was sinking to the bottom of the sea.

94
00:06:15.936 --> 00:06:20.360
Tormented by nightmares, I tossed and turned before finally falling into a deep sleep.

95
00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:23.962
When I awoke I didn't know how long we'd been sleeping.

96
00:06:24.683 --> 00:06:28.386
We must have used up most of the air in the cabin because it was difficult to breathe.

97
00:06:29.847 --> 00:06:34.651
Just as I was speculating about how the captain refreshed the air for the whole vessel, I smelled

98
00:06:34.691 --> 00:06:35.551
a sea breeze.

99
00:06:36.612 --> 00:06:41.216
The ship rolled, and I realized that it had risen to the surface to breathe like a whale.

100
00:06:42.288 --> 00:06:45.990
Ned and Conseil awoke and breathed in the pure, fresh air.

101
00:06:46.010 --> 00:06:47.890
"What time is it?"

102
00:06:48.211 --> 00:06:48.671
asked Ned.

103
00:06:48.691 --> 00:06:51.372
"I have no idea," I replied.

104
00:06:51.392 --> 00:06:54.393
"It's possible that we've slept for 24 hours."

105
00:06:55.474 --> 00:06:57.735
More time passed and Ned grew angry.

106
00:06:58.455 --> 00:07:01.076
There was no sound on the boat, and it didn't move.

107
00:07:02.016 --> 00:07:06.718
I felt terrified; Conseil remained calm; Ned roared with anger and hunger.

108
00:07:07.699 --> 00:07:10.120
Suddenly the door opened and the steward appeared.

109
00:07:10.796 --> 00:07:13.057
With a shout Ned threw the man to the floor.

110
00:07:13.978 --> 00:07:15.959
Conseil tried to pull Ned off the steward.

111
00:07:16.659 --> 00:07:21.081
I was just about to jump into the fray when I was stopped by hearing these words in

112
00:07:21.161 --> 00:07:23.342
French: "Be quiet, Mr.

113
00:07:23.402 --> 00:07:27.864
Land, and you, Professor Aronnax, will you kindly listen to me?"
